A||||2||Buster||coolant crossover removal & hoses||||1||
Z||000000||Buster||08-21-2000||09:49 PM||||For an 88 V6 auto, with AC. I am replacing all five cooling hoses. I would like to remove the crossover tube. I have both hoses off and both retaining bolts out. What else has to be removed to allow the tube to come out. I want to stop the rust on it. As a side question, in the area of the top mounting bolt there is a bracket clamped to the two fuel lines, but the other end is not atached and I can not tell where it goes. The bracket has a twist and the loose end has a sort of a "J" shape to it. Last question, on replacing heater hoses. <BR>Can the crimped rubber hoses be removed from the steel lines and replaced with conventional hose and clamps. I have the two hoses for the front that atach that way. I would like to replace the other ends around the motor. I am going through the cooling system as complete as I can.<P>Thanks for any and all help.   Buster ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodney||08-22-2000||02:57 PM||||I have new reproduced stainless steel coolant crossover tubes for the V-6. The OEM ones rust from the inside out and the outside in. Mine are 304 stainless steel and will never rust. On my web page:<P>  <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||08-22-2000||05:42 PM||||Buster,<BR>Have fun getting that crossover out. I really "exercised my vocabulary" when I replaced mine.<BR>If memory serves... You have to drop the top compressor bracket (remove the belt, and let the compressor fall toward the firewall), and a few other things to get THAT loose. You'll need to remove the starter. You'll need to remove some ground wires that attach to the "front" cylinder head, just above the trans. They will hold the wiring harness in the way. Unplug the O2 sensor and the TCC connector (automatics.) I left my exhaust system in place, but my cat is smaller than stock. <BR>When you put it back together, make sure that the wiring harness is nowhere near the exhaust manifold.<P>BTW... If you don't know Rodney, he produces some of the nicest Fiero stuff in the industry. I've seen his crossovers. Would have bought it when I did mine if it had been available. (It's also not any more expensive than GM's replacement was, --before GM quit making it.)<BR>Have fun!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>When I die, I want to go peacefully, in my sleep, like Grandpa.<BR>Not screaming, like the passengers in his car.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-22-2000).]||||reg||1
